Write-up for PA3
----------------


Implementation Overview
-----------------------

The COOL parser was implemented using GNU Bison in cool.y.

The grammar recognizes class declarations, inheritance, attributes,
methods, formal parameters, arithmetic and comparison expressions,
assignments, control-flow expressions, method dispatch, object creation,
let expressions, case expressions, and blocks.

Semantic actions construct the COOL abstract syntax tree (AST) using
the constructors provided by the assignment framework.

Design Decisions
----------------

Operator precedence and associativity were explicitly declared to
resolve ambiguities and preserve the intended interpretation of COOL
expressions.

Lists of classes, features, formals, arguments, block expressions,
and case branches are constructed using the corresponding AST list
constructors.

Multiple let declarations are represented as nested let expressions,
preserving their original declaration order and scope.

Source line tracking uses Bison locations and the node_lineno
mechanism. Missing initialization expressions are represented by
no_expr() nodes with line number zero, matching the reference parser.

Syntax Error Recovery
---------------------

Error recovery productions were implemented for:

- Class declarations
- Class features (attributes and methods)
- Let declarations
- Block expressions

These productions use Bison's error token and synchronization
delimiters to continue parsing after syntax errors.

Previously recognized AST elements are preserved where appropriate.
Invalid programs are rejected after their errors have been reported.

Testing and Validation
----------------------

good.cl contains valid COOL programs covering the implemented
language constructs, including nested expressions, operator
precedence, dispatch, let declarations, and case expressions.

bad.cl contains invalid programs covering malformed classes,
attributes, methods, let declarations, and block expressions.

Additional isolated recovery tests were used during development.

The complete AST generated for good.cl was compared with the
official COOL parser using diff. Both outputs were identical.

The error diagnostics generated for the isolated recovery tests
were also identical to those of the official parser.

The consolidated bad.cl produced the same 13 diagnostics reported
by the official parser, with one additional EOF diagnostic caused
by an intentionally incomplete final class.

Bison conflict reports were checked during development, and the
final grammar produced no reported parsing conflicts in the tests.

Conclusion
----------

The implemented parser successfully recognizes the tested valid
COOL constructions, generates the expected AST, and provides
syntax error recovery for the tested invalid constructions.

Comparisons against the official parser support the correctness
of the implementation for the supplied test cases.
